weet spot failed)\n\nSo roughly 5 of 13 experiment groups were completed; 1 incomplete; 7 not attempted.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the paper on methodology and experiments. Score on a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept), targeting ~25% accept rate at a top ML venue.\n\nAddress these specific questions in your review:\n\n### Methodology\n1. Is the STA/ISR formulation sound? Any edge cases or failure modes not addressed?\n2. Is the multiplicative reward formulation r = r_exec · (1 + β · ISR) well-justified? Could alternative formulations (additive, etc.) yield different conclusions?\n3. Is the random control (uniform noise) an appropriate baseline? Are there better controls?\n4. Is the hacking taxonomy well-defined and exhaustive? How are hacking modes classified—manually or automatically?\n\n### Experiments\n5. 3 seeds is the minimum for any variance claim. Is this sufficient given the extreme seed sensitivity observed (CV=1.32)?\n6. β=0.3 is tested on only 1 seed—can any conclusion be drawn?\n7. The ISR collapse trajectory (Table 2) is only seed 42. Is this representative?\n8. Eval on 100 problems—is this sufficient for statistical claims?\n9. The Prolog experiment is incomplete (180/500 steps). Does presenting it add value or just pad the paper?\n10. Missing experiments: SFT baseline, O(1) vs O(N) STA, larger models, more β values, more seeds. How much does each absence weaken the claims?\n11. Are training curves / learning dynamics shown anywhere? The paper relies heavily on endpoint metrics.\n\n### Statistical Rigor\n12. \"p > 0.5\" for paired t-test with n=3—is this test even meaningful at n=3?\n13. The paper claims \"no directional consistency\"—with 3 data points, is this a statistical or anecdotal claim?\n\nFormat your review as:\n```\n## Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n## Strengths\n[Bulleted list]\n\n## Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, numbered W1-Wn]\n\n## Questions for Authors\n[Bulleted list]\n\n## Missing Experiments Assessment\n[Which missin