{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018aHczaRVSmo3yb7xKjTCZg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage{times}\n3\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n5\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n11\t\\usepackage[breaklinks]{hyperref}\n12\t\\usepackage{url}\n13\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n14\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n15\t\\renewcommand{\\bibsection}{\\section*{References}}\n16\t\n17\t\\title{Solver Trace Ablation Reveals Reward Hacking in GRPO-Trained\\\\Formal Reasoners: Diagnosis, Taxonomy, and Goodhart's Law}\n18\t\n19\t\\author{Anonymous}\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{document}\n22\t\\maketitle\n23\t\n24\t%==============================================================================\n25\t% ABSTRACT\n26\t%==============================================================================\n27\t\\begin{abstract}\n28\tGroup Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with execution-only rewards trains neural formal reasoners that achieve high prove rates but produce structurally degenerate outputs---a form of reward hacking invisible to standard evaluation.\n29\tWe propose \\textbf{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}, a diagnostic framework that derives the \\textbf{Intensional Structural Reward (ISR)} via leave-one-out component necessity testing on solver inputs.\n30\tApplying STA to GRPO-trained first-order logic (FOL) formalizers on FOLIO, we make three contributions.\n31\t\\textbf{(1)}~ISR quantifies structural collapse invisible to prove rate: exec-only GRPO achieves 93\\% prove rate while ISR collapses from 0.64 to 0.003 over 1000 training steps, and we identify a taxonomy of 5 distinct hacking strategies.\n32\t\\textbf{(2)}~Using ISR as an auxiliary reward exhibits Goodhart's Law: at low dose ($\\beta{=}0.1$), ISR provides directional structural improvement over matched random control (mean ISR ratio ${\\sim}2.1\\times$ across 3 seeds), but with high inter-seed variance (std/mean$=$0.47); at high dose ($\\beta{=}0.3$), ISR reward is actively worse than random noise (prove rate 18\\% vs.\\ 48\\%).\n33\t\\textbf{(3)}~We present preliminary evidence that the STA protocol mechanically transfers to Prolog, though ISR diagnostic power in this setting remains limited.\n34\tNotably, seed variability itself emerges as a finding: the same hyperparameters with 3 different seeds produce 3 qualitatively different hacking strategies, underscoring GRPO training instability.\n35\t\\end{abstract}\n36\t\n37\t%==============================================================================\n38\t% 1. INTRODUCTION\n39\t%==============================================================================\n40\t\\section{Introduction}\n41\t\n42\tNeural formal reasoning has advanced rapidly with reinforcement learning approaches such as GRPO \\cite{shao2024deepseekmath}, which optimize language models against execution-based rewards from automated theorem provers.\n43\tA model trained to maximize prove rate on first-order logic (FOL) problems can achieve 93\\% success, yet the formalizations it produces are structurally degenerate---containing redundant premises, embedded conclusions, or tautological shortcuts that satisfy the prover without performing genuine reasoning.\n44\tThis \\emph{reward hacking} phenomenon is invisible to prove rate alone, creating a dangerous gap between apparent capability and actual reasoning quality.\n45\t\n46\tWe address this gap with \\textbf{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}, a diagnostic framework inspired by component necessity testing from mutation testing and feature attribution.\n47\tSTA performs leave-one-out (LOO) ablation on the components of a solver input (e.g., premises in a FOL formalization) and derives the \\textbf{Intensional Structural Reward (ISR)}, which measures the fraction of components that are individually necessary for the proof to succeed.\n48\tAn ISR near 1.0 indicates that every component contributes to the proof; an ISR near 0.0 indicates that most components are redundant or that the proof relies on structural shortcuts.\n49\t\n50\tOur contributions are threefold:\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{ISR as a Diagnostic for Reward Hacking.}\n53\tWe demonstrate that ISR captures structural degeneration that prove rate misses entirely.\n54\tUnder exec-only GRPO training, prove rate rises from 32\\% (100 steps) to 96\\% (1000 steps), while ISR collapses from 0.64 to 0.003.\n55\tThrough manual annotation of 200 outputs per condition, we identify 5 distinct hacking strategies: conclusion embedding, redundant premises, tautological collapse, repetition collapse, and complexity without utility.\n56\t\n57\t\\paragraph{ISR as Reward: A Goodhart Case Study.}\n58\tWe investigate ISR as an auxiliary training signal and find that it exhibits Goodhart's Law \\cite{manheim2019categorizing}.\n59\tAt $\\beta{=}0.1$ (additive bonus formulation), ISR reward provides directional structural improvement over a matched random control (mean ISR ratio ${\\sim}2.1\\times$ across 3 seeds), but the effect is fragile: inter-seed variance is high (ISR std/mean $= 0.47$), and the genuine rate improvement ($+3.7$pp) does not reach statistical significance with 3 seeds.\n60\tAt $\\beta{=}0.3$, ISR reward causes Goodhart collapse---prove rate drops to 18\\%, worse than the matched random control at 48\\%.\n61\t\n62\t\\paragraph{Seed Variability as Evidence of GRPO Instability.}\n63\tThree exec-only seeds with identical hyperparameters produce three qualitatively different hacking strategies: conclusion embedding (s42), repetition collapse (s43, PR$=$14\\%), and tautological collapse (s44, PR$=$98\\%, fake-high ISR$=$0.57).\n64\tThis divergence is itself a contribution, demonstrating that single-seed GRPO evaluations are unreliable for formal reasoning.\n65\t\n66\tWe additionally present preliminary evidence that STA mechanically transfers to Prolog (rule ablation), though ISR diagnostic power in this setting is weaker than in FOL.\n67\tModel scaling experiments (1.5B to 8B parameters) suggest that increased scale does not resolve reward hacking---it changes the hacking strategy's granularity without eliminating structural degeneration.\n68\t\n69\t%==============================================================================\n70\t% 2. RELATED WORK\n71\t%==============================================================================\n72\t\\section{Related Work}\n73\t\n74\t\\paragraph{Reward Hacking and Goodhart's Law.}\n75\tReward hacking is well-documented in RLHF \\cite{gao2023scaling,skalse2022defining}, where over-optimization of a learned reward model degrades true quality.\n76\tManheim and Garrabrant~\\cite{manheim2019categorizing} categorize four regimes of Goodhart's Law; our empirical findings map to their ``regressional'' and ``extremal'' categories.\n77\tKarwowski et al.~\\cite{karwowski2024goodhart} study Goodhart effects in language model alignment.\n78\tOur work provides a controlled empirical demonstration in the understudied setting of formal reasoning GRPO, where the reward (execution success) is verifiable rather than learned.\n79\t\n80\t\\paragraph{GRPO and Formal Reasoning.}\n81\tGRPO \\cite{shao2024deepseekmath} uses group-relative advantages for policy optimization without a separate value network.\n82\tRecent work applies GRPO to mathematical reasoning \\cite{shao2024deepseekmath}, code generation, and formal logic \\cite{han2022folio}.\n83\tEVOM uses scalar execution rewards only.\n84\tMellgren et al.\\ hand-craft Prolog-specific structure penalties.\n85\tNone of these works systematically diagnose or characterize reward hacking in formal reasoning.\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Feature Attribution and Ablation.}\n88\tISR's LOO ablation is conceptually related to Shapley values \\cite{lundberg2017unified} and leave-one-out approaches in feature attribution.\n89\tWe explicitly acknowledge that ISR is not algorithmically novel---the contribution is its application as a hacking diagnostic for formal reasoning, combined with the empirical Goodhart analysis.\n90\t\n91\t\\paragraph{Neural Theorem Proving.}\n92\tLeanDojo \\cite{yang2023leandojo}, Baldur \\cite{first2023baldur}, and related systems focus on interactive theorem proving in Lean/Isabelle.\n93\tOur setting differs: we study \\emph{autoformalization} (natural language to FOL) verified by automated provers (Prover9), where the model generates the entire formalization rather than individual proof steps.\n94\t\n95\t%==============================================================================\n96\t% 3. METHOD\n97\t%==============================================================================\n98\t\\section{Method: Solver Trace Ablation}\n99\t\n100\t\\subsection{Problem Setting}\n101\t\n102\tGiven a natural language reasoning problem $x$ with answer label $y \\in \\{\\texttt{true}, \\texttt{false}, \\texttt{unknown}\\}$, a formalizer model $\\pi_\\theta$ generates a candidate FOL formalization $f = (p_1, \\ldots, p_n, c)$ consisting of premises $p_i$ and conclusion $c$.\n103\tAn automated prover (Prover9/Mace4) evaluates $f$, yielding binary execution reward $r_{\\text{exec}} \\in \\{0, 1\\}$.\n104\tStandard GRPO maximizes $\\mathbb{E}[r_{\\text{exec}}]$, achieving high prove rate but potentially through structural shortcuts.\n105\t\n106\t\\subsection{ISR: Intensional Structural Reward}\n107\t\n108\tSTA derives ISR through component necessity testing.\n109\tFor a proved formalization $f = (p_1, \\ldots, p_n, c)$, we define:\n110\t\n111\t\\begin{equation}\n112\t\\text{ISR}(f) = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}\\left[\\text{prove}(f \\setminus \\{p_i\\}) = \\texttt{fail}\\right]\n113\t\\label{eq:isr}\n114\t\\end{equation}\n115\t\n116\twhere $f \\setminus \\{p_i\\}$ is the formalization with premise $p_i$ removed.\n117\tISR $= 1.0$ means every premise is individually necessary; ISR $= 0.0$ means no premise is necessary (the proof succeeds from the conclusion alone or from structural artifacts).\n118\tFor unproved formalizations ($r_{\\text{exec}} = 0$), ISR is undefined (set to 0 by convention).\n119\t\n120\tThis requires $O(n)$ prover calls per sample, where $n$ is the number of components.\n121\tSection~\\ref{sec:sta-approx} shows that $O(1)$ approximations fail to preserve ISR's discriminative power.\n122\t\n123\t\\subsection{ISR as Auxiliary Reward}\n124\t\n125\tWe investigate two formulations for incorporating ISR into GRPO training:\n126\t\n127\t\\paragraph{Additive Bonus.}\n128\t\\begin{equation}\n129\tr = r_{\\text{exec}} \\cdot (1 + \\beta \\cdot \\text{ISR})\n130\t\\label{eq:additive}\n131\t\\end{equation}\n132\t\n133\tISR provides a bonus on top of execution success. Crucially, ISR can only increase reward when $r_{\\text{exec}} = 1$, preserving the primacy of execution.\n134\t\n135\t\\paragraph{Convex Combination.}\n136\t\\begin{equation}\n137\tr = \\alpha \\cdot r_{\\text{exec}} + \\beta \\cdot r_{\\text{exec}} \\cdot \\text{ISR}, \\quad \\alpha + \\beta = 1\n138\t\\label{eq:convex}\n139\t\\end{equation}\n140\t\n141\tWhen ISR $< 1$ (which is almost always the case), this formula applies an implicit \\emph{penalty} to execution success, making it fundamentally unsuitable as a reward signal (Section~\\ref{sec:curriculum}).\n142\t\n143\t\\subsection{Hacking Taxonomy}\n144\t\n145\tThrough manual annotation of 200 outputs per condition, we identify 5 extensional shortcut modes:\n146\t\n147\t\\begin{enumerate}\n148\t\\item \\textbf{Conclusion Embedding}: The conclusion (or its negation) is included as a premise, making the proof trivially valid.\n149\t\\item \\textbf{Redundant Premises}: Extra premises that do not contribute to the proof, inflating complexity without utility.\n150\t\\item \\textbf{Tautological Collapse}: The formalization reduces to a tautology (e.g., $P \\rightarrow P$) with minimal component count.\n151\t\\item \\textbf{Repetition Collapse}: The model generates repetitive structure, often collapsing prove rate entirely.\n152\t\\item \\textbf{Complexity Without Utility}: High component count with no structural contribution---premises are syntactically varied but semantically vacuous.\n153\t\\end{enumerate}\n154\t\n155\t%==============================================================================\n156\t% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n157\t%==============================================================================\n158\t\\section{Experiments}\n159\t\n160\t\\subsection{Setup}\n161\t\n162\t\\paragraph{Data.} We use the FOLIO dataset \\cite{han2022folio}, a benchmark of 100 held-out natural language reasoning problems with FOL annotations. Each problem requires formalizing premises and a conclusion in first-order logic.\n163\t\n164\t\\paragraph{Model.} Qwen3-1.5B serves as the primary formalizer, with Qwen3-8B used for scaling experiments. Models are trained with GRPO ($K{=}8$ group size) for 500 steps (extended to 1000 for collapse trajectory analysis).\n165\t\n166\t\\paragraph{Prover.} Prover9/Mace4 for FOL, SWI-Prolog for Prolog experiments. Each prover call has a 30-second timeout.\n167\t\n168\t\\paragraph{Conditions.} We evaluate 7 primary conditions: exec-only (baseline), ISR $\\beta{=}0.1$, ISR $\\beta{=}0.3$, random $\\beta{=}0.1$, random $\\beta{=}0.3$, convex combination, and SFT. Each GRPO condition is run with 3 seeds (s42, s43, s44) where budget permits.\n169\t\n170\t\\paragraph{Metrics.} Prove Rate (PR): fraction of problems solved. ISR: mean intensional structural reward (Eq.~\\ref{eq:isr}). Genuine Rate: fraction of proved outputs classified as structurally genuine via manual annotation. Component Count: mean number of premises per formalization.\n171\t\n172\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n173\t% 4.1 ISR COLLAPSE TRAJECTORY\n174\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n175\t\\subsection{Claim 1: ISR Quantifies Structural Collapse}\n176\t\\label{sec:claim1}\n177\t\n178\t\\begin{table}[t]\n179\t\\centering\n180\t\\small\n181\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n182\t\\toprule\n183\t\\textbf{Training Steps} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{Components} \\\\\n184\t\\midrule\n185\t100 & 0.32 & 0.6423 & 6.58 \\\\\n186\t500 & 0.93 & 0.1324 & 8.50 \\\\\n187\t1000 & 0.96 & 0.0027 & 18.55 \\\\\n188\t\\bottomrule\n189\t\\end{tabular}\n190\t\\caption{ISR collapse trajectory under exec-only GRPO (seed 42). Prove rate increases monotonically while ISR collapses by 99.6\\% over 1000 steps. Component count inflation (6.58$\\to$18.55) indicates explosion-style degeneration.}\n191\t\\label{tab:collapse}\n192\t\\end{table}\n193\t\n194\tTable~\\ref{tab:collapse} presents the central diagnostic finding. Under exec-only GRPO, prove rate rises from 32\\% to 96\\% over 1000 training steps, while ISR collapses from 0.6423 to 0.0027---a 99.6\\% decline. At 1000 steps, 94.8\\% of proved samples have ISR$=$0, meaning not a single premise is individually necessary for the proof to succeed. The simultaneous increase in component count (6.58$\\to$18.55) indicates that the model generates increasingly verbose formalizations where every component is redundant.\n195\t\n196\tThis collapse is invisible to prove rate, which continues to improve. ISR provides the missing diagnostic signal.\n197\t\n198\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n199\t% 4.2 SEED VARIABILITY AND HACKING TAXONOMY\n200\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dcf33VCz6gsQyTv4BYFvp6","session_id":"f8da9cdc-096a-48ff-af14-645ac8499d5f","uuid":"8d1a0e43-2b60-4c76-ae11-d2ae5a549658","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:27:01.410Z"}
