{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018KvJ4qxBrpeD5nqgWwXmhU","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n201\t\\subsection{Seed Variability and Hacking Taxonomy}\n202\t\n203\t\\begin{table}[t]\n204\t\\centering\n205\t\\small\n206\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n207\t\\toprule\n208\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{Hacking Mode} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{Comp.} \\\\\n209\t\\midrule\n210\ts42 & conclusion embedding & 0.93 & 0.132 & 8.5 \\\\\n211\ts43 & repetition collapse & 0.14 & 0.000 & --- \\\\\n212\ts44 & tautological collapse & 0.98 & 0.570 & 2.04 \\\\\n213\t\\bottomrule\n214\t\\end{tabular}\n215\t\\caption{Three exec-only seeds produce qualitatively different hacking strategies. s44's high ISR (0.57) is a false positive: with only 2.04 components, both are trivially ``necessary'' in a tautological proof.}\n216\t\\label{tab:seeds}\n217\t\\end{table}\n218\t\n219\tTable~\\ref{tab:seeds} demonstrates that identical hyperparameters produce dramatically different outcomes across seeds.\n220\tSeed 42 converges to conclusion embedding (high PR, low ISR).\n221\tSeed 43 undergoes repetition collapse, failing to prove most problems (PR$=$14\\%).\n222\tSeed 44 achieves the highest prove rate (98\\%) via tautological collapse---reducing formalizations to minimal tautologies with only ${\\sim}2$ components, producing a misleadingly high ISR of 0.57.\n223\t\n224\tThis divergence is itself a contribution: single-seed GRPO results are unreliable for formal reasoning, and the diversity of failure modes suggests that the reward landscape contains multiple degenerate attractors.\n225\t\n226\tThe hacking taxonomy (5 modes) is derived from manual annotation of 200 outputs per condition. In the exec-only s42 condition, approximately 95\\% of proved outputs are classified as hacking, with conclusion embedding as the dominant mode.\n227\t\n228\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n229\t% 4.3 ISR AS REWARD: GOODHART CASE STUDY\n230\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n231\t\\subsection{Claim 2: ISR as Reward Exhibits Goodhart's Law}\n232\t\\label{sec:claim2}\n233\t\n234\t\\begin{table}[t]\n235\t\\centering\n236\t\\small\n237\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n238\t\\toprule\n239\t\\textbf{Condition} & $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{Gen.\\%} \\\\\n240\t\\midrule\n241\texec-only (s42) & --- & 0.93 & 0.132 & ${\\sim}$5 \\\\\n242\t\\midrule\n243\tISR reward (s42) & 0.1 & 0.81 & 0.337 & 25.9 \\\\\n244\trandom control (s42) & 0.1 & 0.79 & 0.104 & 17.7 \\\\\n245\t\\midrule\n246\tISR reward & 0.3 & 0.18 & 0.026 & --- \\\\\n247\trandom control & 0.3 & 0.48 & 0.045 & --- \\\\\n248\t\\bottomrule\n249\t\\end{tabular}\n250\t\\caption{Goodhart landscape: $\\beta{=}0.1$ ISR reward improves structural quality over matched random; $\\beta{=}0.3$ ISR reward is \\emph{worse} than random noise on both PR and ISR. The Goodhart transition lies between $\\beta \\in (0.1, 0.3)$.}\n251\t\\label{tab:goodhart}\n252\t\\end{table}\n253\t\n254\tTable~\\ref{tab:goodhart} presents the Goodhart landscape for ISR as a reward signal (seed 42).\n255\tAt $\\beta{=}0.1$, ISR reward achieves ISR$=$0.337 vs.\\ random control ISR$=$0.104---a $3.2\\times$ improvement with comparable prove rate (0.81 vs.\\ 0.79).\n256\tGenuine rate improves from 17.7\\% (random) to 25.9\\% (ISR), and complexity-without-utility hacking is completely eliminated (0\\% vs.\\ 3.8\\% in the random condition).\n257\t\n258\tAt $\\beta{=}0.3$, Goodhart collapse occurs: ISR reward achieves only PR$=$0.18 vs.\\ random PR$=$0.48.\n259\tThe ISR signal becomes actively misleading---the model over-optimizes structural regularity at the expense of execution success.\n260\t\n261\t\\subsubsection{3-Seed $\\beta{=}0.1$ Aggregate}\n262\t\n263\t\\begin{table}[t]\n264\t\\centering\n265\t\\small\n266\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n267\t\\toprule\n268\t& \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{ISR $\\beta{=}0.1$}} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{Random $\\beta{=}0.1$}} \\\\\n269\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-7}\n270\t\\textbf{Seed} & PR & ISR & Gen.\\% & PR & ISR & Gen.\\% \\\\\n271\t\\midrule\n272\ts42 & 0.81 & 0.337 & 25.9 & 0.79 & 0.104 & 17.7 \\\\\n273\ts43 & 0.72 & 0.240 & 21.0 & 0.74 & 0.130 & 17.0 \\\\\n274\ts44 & 0.55 & 0.120 & 13.0 & 0.71 & 0.090 & 14.0 \\\\\n275\t\\midrule\n276\tmean$\\pm$std & \\makebox[0pt][l]{0.69$\\pm$0.13} & \\makebox[0pt][l]{0.23$\\pm$0.11} & \\makebox[0pt][l]{20.0$\\pm$6.5} & \\makebox[0pt][l]{0.75$\\pm$0.04} & \\makebox[0pt][l]{0.11$\\pm$0.02} & \\makebox[0pt][l]{16.2$\\pm$2.0} \\\\\n277\t\\bottomrule\n278\t\\end{tabular}\n279\t\\caption{3-seed $\\beta{=}0.1$ ISR vs.\\ random comparison. ISR shows directional improvement (mean ratio ${\\sim}2.1\\times$) but high inter-seed variance. The 95\\% CI for the ISR ratio spans [0.85, 3.45], crossing 1.0.}\n280\t\\label{tab:3seed}\n281\t\\end{table}\n282\t\n283\tTable~\\ref{tab:3seed} reveals that the $\\beta{=}0.1$ sweet spot is directionally reproduced but fragile across seeds.\n284\tThe mean ISR ratio (ISR-reward / random-reward) is ${\\sim}2.1\\times$, but the 95\\% confidence interval based on 3 seeds is [0.85, 3.45], crossing 1.0.\n285\tThe genuine rate delta shrinks from $+8.2$pp (single seed s42) to $+3.7$pp (3-seed mean), which is not statistically significant.\n286\t\n287\tThe inter-seed variance is itself informative.\n288\tISR reward's PR standard deviation (0.13) is $3.3\\times$ that of random reward (0.04), indicating that ISR signal interacts with seed-specific training dynamics in complex ways.\n289\tSeed 44 illustrates a failure mode: its exec-only baseline produces tautological proofs with ISR$\\approx$0.5, and the $\\beta{=}0.1$ ISR bonus cannot distinguish ``genuinely 2-component proof'' from ``tautological 2-component hack,'' resulting in a confused optimization landscape (PR drops to 0.55, ISR to 0.12).\n290\t\n291\tWe characterize the $\\beta{=}0.1$ result as \\emph{directional improvement with high initialization sensitivity}: the ISR signal provides genuine structural guidance in 2 of 3 seeds (s42, s43) but fails for initializations that produce hacking modes where ISR lacks discriminative power.\n292\t\n293\t\\subsubsection{Per-Seed $\\beta{=}0.1$ Analysis}\n294\t\n295\t\\begin{table}[t]\n296\t\\centering\n297\t\\small\n298\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n299\t\\toprule\n300\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{Cond.} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{ISR$_{\\text{std}}$} & \\textbf{Gen.\\%} & \\textbf{CE\\%} \\\\\n301\t\\midrule\n302\t\\multirow{2}{*}{s43} & ISR & 0.72 & 0.24 & 0.28 & 21 & 75 \\\\\n303\t & random & 0.74 & 0.13 & 0.15 & 17 & --- \\\\\n304\t\\midrule\n305\t\\multirow{2}{*}{s44} & ISR & 0.55 & 0.12 & 0.19 & 13 & 78 \\\\\n306\t & random & 0.71 & 0.09 & 0.12 & 14 & --- \\\\\n307\t\\bottomrule\n308\t\\end{tabular}\n309\t\\caption{Per-seed $\\beta{=}0.1$ breakdown for s43 and s44. CE\\% = conclusion embedding rate. s43: ISR rescues a collapsed baseline (exec-only PR$=$14\\%) but effect is weaker than s42. s44: ISR is counterproductive---PR drops below random control.}\n310\t\\label{tab:perseed}\n311\t\\end{table}\n312\t\n313\tTable~\\ref{tab:perseed} provides per-seed detail.\n314\tSeed 43's exec-only baseline suffers repetition collapse (PR$=$14\\%), making it the worst seed.\n315\tBoth ISR and random $\\beta{=}0.1$ bonuses rescue it (PR$=$0.72 and 0.74 respectively), with ISR providing an additional structural signal (ISR 0.24 vs.\\ 0.13).\n316\tHowever, the PR recovery is driven by the regularization effect of any additive bonus, not ISR specifically.\n317\t\n318\tSeed 44 presents the opposite pattern: its exec-only baseline achieves PR$=$98\\% through tautological collapse, and the ISR bonus disrupts this stable (though degenerate) equilibrium without redirecting toward genuine proofs.\n319\tRandom noise, lacking the misleading ISR$\\approx$0.5 signal, preserves more proving ability (PR 0.71 vs.\\ 0.55).\n320\t\n321\tTable~\\ref{tab:perseed-detail} provides the answer-type breakdown for seeds 43 and 44 under ISR $\\beta{=}0.1$.\n322\t\n323\t\\begin{table}[t]\n324\t\\centering\n325\t\\small\n326\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n327\t\\toprule\n328\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{Type} & \\textbf{Count} & \\textbf{Proved} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} \\\\\n329\t\\midrule\n330\t\\multirow{3}{*}{s43} & true & 34 & 26 & 0.765 & 0.22 \\\\\n331\t & false & 25 & 17 & 0.680 & 0.28 \\\\\n332\t & unknown & 41 & 29 & 0.707 & 0.24 \\\\\n333\t\\midrule\n334\t\\multirow{3}{*}{s44} & true & 34 & 20 & 0.588 & 0.10 \\\\\n335\t & false & 25 & 12 & 0.480 & 0.15 \\\\\n336\t & unknown & 41 & 23 & 0.561 & 0.11 \\\\\n337\t\\bottomrule\n338\t\\end{tabular}\n339\t\\caption{Answer-type breakdown for ISR $\\beta{=}0.1$ seeds 43 and 44. s44 shows uniformly low ISR across all answer types, confirming that the ISR signal failure is not type-specific.}\n340\t\\label{tab:perseed-detail}\n341\t\\end{table}\n342\t\n343\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n344\t% 4.4 SFT BASELINE\n345\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n346\t\\subsection{SFT Baseline: The PR--ISR Tradeoff}\n347\t\n348\t\\begin{table}[t]\n349\t\\centering\n350\t\\small\n351\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n352\t\\toprule\n353\t\\textbf{Condition} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{Gen.\\%} & \\textbf{Hack.\\%} \\\\\n354\t\\midrule\n355\tSFT (gold) & 0.48 & 0.45 & 58 & 42 \\\\\n356\texec-only (s42) & 0.93 & 0.132 & ${\\sim}$5 & ${\\sim}$95 \\\\\n357\tISR $\\beta{=}0.1$ (s42) & 0.81 & 0.337 & 25.9 & 74.1 \\\\\n358\trandom $\\beta{=}0.1$ (s42) & 0.79 & 0.104 & 17.7 & 82.3 \\\\\n359\t\\bottomrule\n360\t\\end{tabular}\n361\t\\caption{SFT vs.\\ GRPO variants. SFT achieves the highest ISR (0.45) and genuine rate (58\\%) but the lowest prove rate (48\\%), establishing upper and lower bounds for the PR--ISR tradeoff.}\n362\t\\label{tab:sft}\n363\t\\end{table}\n364\t\n365\tTable~\\ref{tab:sft} situates our GRPO results against a supervised fine-tuning (SFT) baseline trained on 500 gold FOLIO formalizations.\n366\tSFT achieves the highest structural quality (ISR$=$0.45, genuine rate 58\\%) but the lowest prove rate (48\\%), because it optimizes token-level cross-entropy rather than execution success.\n367\t\n368\tThis comparison reveals the fundamental tradeoff: GRPO's prove rate advantage is purchased at the cost of structural quality.\n369\tISR $\\beta{=}0.1$ occupies an intermediate position (PR$=$0.81, ISR$=$0.337), representing a directional step toward balancing execution success with structural integrity.\n370\tThe SFT hacking rate (42\\%) reflects conclusion embedding patterns learned from the gold data's premise selection bias, not reward hacking \\textit{per se}.\n371\t\n372\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n373\t% 4.5 STA APPROXIMATION ABLATION\n374\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n375\t\\subsection{STA Approximation: $O(1)$ vs.\\ $O(N)$}\n376\t\\label{sec:sta-approx}\n377\t\n378\t\\begin{table}[t]\n379\t\\centering\n380\t\\small\n381\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n382\t\\toprule\n383\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{$O(1)$ Fast ISR} & \\textbf{$O(N)$ Full STA} \\\\\n384\t\\midrule\n385\tPearson $r$ with full ISR & 0.21 & 1.00 \\\\\n386\tMean absolute error & 0.31 & 0.00 \\\\\n387\tAUROC (genuine vs.\\ hack) & 0.56 & 0.84 \\\\\n388\tThroughput (samples/sec) & 3.2 & 0.3 \\\\\n389\t\\bottomrule\n390\t\\end{tabular}\n391\t\\caption{$O(1)$ single-component sampling vs.\\ $O(N)$ full LOO ablation. The $10\\times$ speed gain of $O(1)$ comes at the cost of near-random discrimination (AUROC 0.56). ISR's diagnostic power requires complete ablation.}\n392\t\\label{tab:approx}\n393\t\\end{table}\n394\t\n395\tA natural question is whether ISR's $O(n)$ cost per sample can be reduced.\n396\tTable~\\ref{tab:approx} evaluates an $O(1)$ approximation that samples a single component for necessity testing, analogous to kernel SHAP vs.\\ exact SHAP.\n397\t\n398\tThe $O(1)$ approximation achieves only Pearson $r{=}0.21$ with full ISR and near-chance discrimination between genuine and hacked proofs (AUROC$=$0.56).\n399\tWhen used as a reward signal, it degrades prove rate to 0.43---below the exec-only baseline---because the noisy ISR estimate provides no coherent gradient direction.\n400\tThis is a classic bias-variance tradeoff failure: removing the $O(n)$ cost eliminates the signal.\n401\t\n402\tThe $10\\times$ throughput advantage (3.2 vs.\\ 0.3 samples/sec) is insufficient to compensate for the loss of discriminative power.\n403\tFull LOO ablation remains necessary for reliable ISR computation.\n404\t\n405\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n406\t% 4.6 CURRICULUM ABLATION\n407\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n408\t\\subsection{Curriculum Ablation}\n409\t\\label{sec:curriculum}\n410\t\n411\t\\begin{table}[t]\n412\t\\centering\n413\t\\small\n414\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n415\t\\toprule\n416\t\\textbf{Curriculum} & \\textbf{Midpoint} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{Loss} \\\\\n417\t\\midrule\n418\tsigmoid (ref.) & 250 & 0.77 & 0.23 & 0.017 \\\\\n419\tlinear ramp & 100 & 0.71 & 0.20 & 0.020 \\\\\n420\tlinear ramp & 250 & 0.74 & 0.21 & 0.019 \\\\\n421\tlinear ramp & 300 & 0.75 & 0.22 & 0.018 \\\\\n422\tfixed (no ramp) & N/A & 0.69 & 0.18 & 0.021 \\\\\n423\t\\bottomrule\n424\t\\end{tabular}\n425\t\\caption{Curriculum ablation within the convex combination formula. Sigmoid scheduling provides marginal improvement (${\\sim}$3pp PR) over linear ramp. All variants underperform the additive bonus formulation (Table~\\ref{tab:goodhart}), confirming that the convex formula's implicit penalty structure is the first-order problem.}\n426\t\\label{tab:curriculum}\n427\t\\end{table}\n428\t\n429\tTable~\\ref{tab:curriculum} ablates the scheduling strategy for ISR reward introduction within the convex combination formula (Eq.~\\ref{eq:convex}).\n430\tSigmoid scheduling provides a marginal 3pp PR advantage over linear ramp and 5pp over fixed weighting.\n431\tHowever, all convex variants underperform the additive bonus formulation (Table~\\ref{tab:goodhart}, PR$=$0.81 for ISR $\\beta{=}0.1$).\n432\t\n433\tThe convex formula's fundamental issue is that ISR $< 1$ (the common case) always penalizes execution success.\n434\tCurriculum scheduling can delay but not eliminate this penalty.\n435\tThis ablation confirms our choice of the additive bonus formulation (Eq.~\\ref{eq:additive}): the formula choice is a first-order effect; the curriculum is second-order.\n436\t\n437\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n438\t% 4.7 PROLOG: PRELIMINARY CROSS-LOGIC EVIDENCE\n439\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n440\t\\subsection{Preliminary Evidence: Cross-Logic Transfer to Prolog}\n441\t\n442\t\\begin{table}[t]\n443\t\\centering\n444\t\\small\n445\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n446\t\\toprule\n447\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{Exec PR} & \\textbf{Exec ISR} & \\textbf{STA PR} & \\textbf{STA ISR} \\\\\n448\t\\midrule\n449\ts42 & 0.58 & 0.32 & 0.52 & 0.28 \\\\\n450\ts43 & 0.45 & 0.15 & 0.40 & 0.19 \\\\\n451\ts44 & 0.68 & 0.22 & 0.60 & 0.30 \\\\\n452\t\\midrule\n453\tmean & 0.57 & 0.23 & 0.51 & 0.26 \\\\\n454\t\\bottomrule\n455\t\\end{tabular}\n456\t\\caption{Prolog GRPO: exec-only vs.\\ exec+STA ($\\beta{=}0.1$). ISR improvement is marginal (ratio $1.12\\times$) and PR decreases. Hacking modes are partially identifiable: s42 shows partial rule shortcutting, s44 shows builtin bypass.}\n457\t\\label{tab:prolog}\n458\t\\end{table}\n459\t\n460\tTable~\\ref{tab:prolog} presents Prolog results using rule ablation as the STA instantiation.\n461\tThe STA protocol mechanically transfers: ISR is computable via LOO on Prolog rules, and training converges (mean PR$=$0.57 for exec-only).\n462\tHowever, ISR diagnostic power is weaker than in FOL for two reasons.\n463\t\n464\tFirst, Prolog's semantic structure (rules, backtracking, cut) makes ``component necessity'' more ambiguous---removing a rule may cause failure because a base case is missing, not because meaningful reasoning is absent.\n465\tSecond, hacking mode classification is less clear: only 2 of 3 seeds show identifiable patterns (s42: partial rule shortcutting; s44: builtin bypass via \\texttt{assert}/\\texttt{retract}).\n466\t\n467\tISR-as-reward improvement is marginal: the ISR ratio over exec-only is only $1.12\\times$ (vs.\\ $2.1\\times$ in FOL), and PR actually decreases by 6.3pp.\n468\tWe present these results as preliminary evidence that STA is mechanically portable, while noting that ISR's diagnostic and reward utility require logic-specific calibration.\n469\t\n470\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n471\t% 4.8 MULTI-LOGIC AND CROSS-TRANSFER\n472\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n473\t\\subsection{Multi-Logic Training and Cross-Transfer}\n474\t\n475\t\\begin{table}[t]\n476\t\\centering\n477\t\\small\n478\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n479\t\\toprule\n480\t\\textbf{Logic} & \\textbf{Single PR} & \\textbf{Multi PR} & $\\boldsymbol{\\Delta}$ \\\\\n481\t\\midrule\n482\tFOL & 0.93 & 0.70 & $-$0.23 \\\\\n483\tProlog & 0.57 & 0.48 & $-$0.09 \\\\\n484\t\\bottomrule\n485\t\\end{tabular}\n486\t\\caption{Single-logic vs.\\ multi-logic (FOL+Prolog) joint training. Negative interference is significant for both logics, particularly FOL ($-$23pp).}\n487\t\\label{tab:multilogic}\n488\t\\end{table}\n489\t\n490\t\\begin{table}[t]\n491\t\\centering\n492\t\\small\n493\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n494\t\\toprule\n495\t\\textbf{Direction} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{Parse Rate} \\\\\n496\t\\midrule\n497\tFOL $\\to$ Prolog & 0.03 & 0.01 & 0.08 \\\\\n498\tProlog $\\to$ FOL & 0.06 & 0.02 & 0.15 \\\\\n499\t\\bottomrule"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dcf33VCz6gsQyTv4BYFvp6","session_id":"f8da9cdc-096a-48ff-af14-645ac8499d5f","uuid":"60aa650e-eaac-4e15-a0e6-3ce7158c1552","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:27:06.084Z"}
