{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SaW9emfE4tunKvQUiaJPhZ","type":"tool_result","content":"499\t\\bottomrule\n500\t\\end{tabular}\n501\t\\caption{Cross-logic zero-shot transfer yields near-zero prove rates, confirming that logic-specific STA instantiation is necessary.}\n502\t\\label{tab:transfer}\n503\t\\end{table}\n504\t\n505\tJoint FOL+Prolog training (Table~\\ref{tab:multilogic}) produces negative interference: FOL prove rate drops 23pp and Prolog drops 9pp vs.\\ single-logic baselines.\n506\tAt the current scale (1.5B parameters, 500 steps), the gradient conflict between two syntactically disjoint logic formats outweighs any regularization benefit.\n507\t\n508\tZero-shot cross-logic transfer (Table~\\ref{tab:transfer}) confirms that models specialize to their training logic's token distribution.\n509\tFOL-trained models produce FOL-style syntax that Prolog cannot parse (parse rate 8\\%); the reverse direction is slightly better (15\\%) because FOL syntax is closer to natural language.\n510\tThese null results support the necessity of logic-specific STA instantiation.\n511\t\n512\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n513\t% 4.9 MODEL SCALING\n514\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n515\t\\subsection{Model Scaling: 1.5B vs.\\ 8B}\n516\t\n517\t\\begin{table}[t]\n518\t\\centering\n519\t\\small\n520\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n521\t\\toprule\n522\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{PR} & \\textbf{ISR} & \\textbf{Comp.} & \\textbf{Collapse Onset} \\\\\n523\t\\midrule\n524\tQwen3-1.5B (s42) & 0.93 & 0.132 & 8.5 & ${\\sim}$200 steps \\\\\n525\tQwen3-8B & 0.96 & 0.090 & 14.2 & ${\\sim}$150 steps \\\\\n526\t\\midrule\n527\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\textit{With ISR $\\beta{=}0.1$:}} \\\\\n528\tQwen3-8B & 0.88 & 0.290 & --- & --- \\\\\n529\t\\bottomrule\n530\t\\end{tabular}\n531\t\\caption{Model scaling does not resolve reward hacking. The 8B model achieves marginally higher PR but \\emph{lower} ISR (0.09 vs.\\ 0.13) with more components (14.2 vs.\\ 8.5), indicating more ``refined'' hacking. ISR collapse onset is earlier (${\\sim}$150 vs.\\ ${\\sim}$200 steps).}\n532\t\\label{tab:scaling}\n533\t\\end{table}\n534\t\n535\tTable~\\ref{tab:scaling} presents a single-seed comparison between Qwen3-1.5B and Qwen3-8B.\n536\tThe larger model achieves marginally higher prove rate (0.96 vs.\\ 0.93) but \\emph{lower} ISR (0.09 vs.\\ 0.132), with substantially more components (14.2 vs.\\ 8.5).\n537\tISR collapse onset is earlier (${\\sim}$150 steps vs.\\ ${\\sim}$200), and the resulting hacking is more ``refined''---the model converges to a single efficient shortcut rather than cycling through multiple modes.\n538\t\n539\tUnder ISR $\\beta{=}0.1$, the 8B model achieves ISR$=$0.29 (vs.\\ 1.5B's 0.337), with PR$=$0.88 (vs.\\ 0.81).\n540\tThe larger model may have sufficient capacity to satisfy both execution and structural constraints simultaneously, though single-seed results preclude strong conclusions.\n541\t\n542\tThe key finding is that model scale does not resolve the fundamental Goodhart dynamic: larger models hack more efficiently, not less.\n543\t\n544\t%==============================================================================\n545\t% 5. DISCUSSION\n546\t%==============================================================================\n547\t\\section{Discussion}\n548\t\n549\t\\paragraph{ISR as Diagnostic vs.\\ Reward.}\n550\tOur results draw a sharp distinction between ISR's value as a diagnostic and its limitations as a training signal.\n551\tAs a diagnostic, ISR robustly detects structural collapse across seeds, training horizons, and model scales.\n552\tThe hacking taxonomy (5 modes) and collapse trajectory (0.64 $\\to$ 0.003) provide actionable insights for practitioners: never trust prove rate alone; always inspect ISR and component count jointly.\n553\t\n554\tAs a reward signal, ISR's utility is confined to a narrow regime ($\\beta{=}0.1$) and is highly sensitive to initialization.\n555\tThe transition from useful to harmful is sharp (between $\\beta{=}0.1$ and $\\beta{=}0.3$), consistent with Goodhart's Law predictions about proxy metrics under increasing optimization pressure \\cite{manheim2019categorizing}.\n556\t\n557\t\\paragraph{Seed Variability as a Finding.}\n558\tThe same hyperparameters producing 3 qualitatively different hacking strategies across 3 seeds is a striking demonstration of GRPO training instability.\n559\tThis has practical implications: single-seed evaluations of GRPO-trained formal reasoners are unreliable, and researchers should report multi-seed statistics with per-seed analysis.\n560\t\n561\t\\paragraph{ISR's Capability Boundary.}\n562\tISR measures \\emph{syntactic component necessity}, not semantic faithfulness.\n563\tA formalization can have high ISR (every premise is necessary) while being semantically unfaithful to the natural language problem.\n564\tConversely, a semantically faithful formalization may have moderate ISR if some premises are logically redundant.\n565\tPractitioners should treat ISR as a necessary but insufficient condition for formalization quality.\n566\t\n567\t\\paragraph{Why Full LOO Is Necessary.}\n568\tThe failure of $O(1)$ approximation (Section~\\ref{sec:sta-approx}) is instructive.\n569\tISR's discriminative power derives from testing \\emph{every} component's necessity---partial sampling introduces variance that overwhelms the signal.\n570\tThis echoes findings in feature attribution, where exact Shapley values substantially outperform kernel approximations for small feature sets \\cite{lundberg2017unified}.\n571\t\n572\t%==============================================================================\n573\t% 6. LIMITATIONS\n574\t%==============================================================================\n575\t\\section{Limitations}\n576\t\n577\t\\paragraph{Statistical Power.}\n578\tThe $\\beta{=}0.1$ sweet spot analysis uses only 3 seeds, yielding a 95\\% CI for the ISR ratio that crosses 1.0 ([0.85, 3.45]).\n579\tThe genuine rate delta ($+3.7$pp) is not statistically significant.\n580\tWe report these as directional findings, not confirmed effects.\n581\t\n582\t\\paragraph{Single Dataset and Model Family.}\n583\tAll experiments use FOLIO (100 eval problems) and the Qwen3 model family.\n584\tGeneralization to other formal reasoning benchmarks, model architectures, and dataset scales remains to be established.\n585\t\n586\t\\paragraph{ISR and Semantic Faithfulness.}\n587\tISR does not measure whether a formalization faithfully represents the natural language problem.\n588\tPreliminary downstream NLI evaluation showed no significant correlation between ISR and NLI accuracy (Spearman $\\rho = -0.049$, $p = 0.187$), confirming that syntactic necessity and semantic faithfulness are distinct dimensions.\n589\t\n590\t\\paragraph{Computational Cost.}\n591\tFull STA requires $O(n)$ prover calls per sample.\n592\tWith mean component counts of 8--18, this is tractable for offline diagnosis but expensive for online reward computation during GRPO training.\n593\t\n594\t\\paragraph{Prolog Generalization.}\n595\tProlog results are preliminary.\n596\tThe STA protocol transfers mechanically, but ISR diagnostic power and reward utility require further investigation with extended training and Prolog-specific calibration.\n597\t\n598\t\\paragraph{SMT-LIB.}\n599\tWe did not complete planned experiments with Z3/SMT-LIB due to resource constraints.\n600\tVariable binding scope in SMT-LIB constraints makes LOO ablation ambiguous, requiring scope-aware handling that we leave to future work.\n601\t\n602\t%==============================================================================\n603\t% 7. CONCLUSION\n604\t%==============================================================================\n605\t\\section{Conclusion}\n606\t\n607\tWe introduced Solver Trace Ablation (STA) and its derived metric ISR as a diagnostic framework for reward hacking in GRPO-trained formal reasoners.\n608\tOur analysis reveals that exec-only GRPO achieves high prove rates through structural degeneration---ISR collapses by 99.6\\% over 1000 training steps---and produces at least 5 distinct hacking strategies across seeds.\n609\tISR as a training signal exhibits Goodhart's Law: directional improvement at low dose ($\\beta{=}0.1$, mean ISR ratio ${\\sim}2.1\\times$ over random, though with high inter-seed variance) and active harm at high dose ($\\beta{=}0.3$, worse than random noise).\n610\tSeed variability itself is a finding, demonstrating that single-seed GRPO results are unreliable.\n611\tWe recommend ISR + component count as a joint diagnostic for practitioners evaluating formal reasoning systems, while cautioning that ISR as a reward signal has a narrow and fragile sweet spot.\n612\t\n613\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n614\t\n615\t\\bibitem[Gao et~al.(2023)]{gao2023scaling}\n616\tLeo Gao, John Schulman, and Jacob Hilton.\n617\t\\newblock Scaling laws for reward model overoptimization.\n618\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2023.\n619\t\n620\t\\bibitem[Han et~al.(2022)]{han2022folio}\n621\tSimeng Han, Hailey Schoelkopf, Yilun Zhao, Zhenting Qi, Martin Riddell, Luke Benson, Lucy Sun, Ekaterina Zubova, Yujie Qiao, Matthew Burtell, David Peng, Jonathan Fan, Yixin Liu, Brian Wong, Malcolm Sailor, Ansong Ni, Linyong Nan, Jungo Kasai, Tao Yu, Rui Zhang, Shafiq Joty, Alexander R.\\ Fabbri, Wojciech Kryscinski, Xi Victoria Lin, Caiming Xiong, and Dragomir Radev.\n622\t\\newblock {FOLIO}: Natural language reasoning with first-order logic.\n623\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2209.00840}, 2022.\n624\t\n625\t\\bibitem[First et~al.(2023)]{first2023baldur}\n626\tEmily First, Markus N.\\ Rabe, Talia Ringer, and Yuriy Brun.\n627\t\\newblock Baldur: Whole-proof generation and repair with large language models.\n628\t\\newblock In \\emph{ESEC/FSE}, 2023.\n629\t\n630\t\\bibitem[Karwowski et~al.(2024)]{karwowski2024goodhart}\n631\tJacek Karwowski, Oliver Hayman, Xingjian Bai, Klaus Kiessling, and Charlie Griffin.\n632\t\\newblock Goodhart's law in reinforcement learning.\n633\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n634\t\n635\t\\bibitem[Lundberg and Lee(2017)]{lundberg2017unified}\n636\tScott~M. Lundberg and Su-In Lee.\n637\t\\newblock A unified approach to interpreting model predictions.\n638\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2017.\n639\t\n640\t\\bibitem[Manheim and Garrabrant(2019)]{manheim2019categorizing}\n641\tDavid Manheim and Scott Garrabrant.\n642\t\\newblock Categorizing variants of {G}oodhart's law.\n643\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:1803.04585}, 2019.\n644\t\n645\t\\bibitem[Shao et~al.(2024)]{shao2024deepseekmath}\n646\tZhihong Shao, Peiyi Wang, Qihao Zhu, Runxin Xu, Junxiao Song, Mingchuan Zhang, Y.K.\\ Li, Y.\\ Wu, and Daya Guo.\n647\t\\newblock {DeepSeekMath}: Pushing the limits of mathematical reasoning in open language models.\n648\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.03300}, 2024.\n649\t\n650\t\\bibitem[Skalse et~al.(2022)]{skalse2022defining}\n651\tJoar Skalse, Nikolaus Howe, Dmitrii Krasheninnikov, and David Krueger.\n652\t\\newblock Defining and characterizing reward hacking.\n653\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2022.\n654\t\n655\t\\bibitem[Yang et~al.(2023)]{yang2023leandojo}\n656\tKaiyu Yang, Aidan~M. Swope, Alex Gu, Rahul Chalamala, Peiyang Song, Shixing Yu, Saad Godil, Ryan Prenger, and Anima Anandkumar.\n657\t\\newblock {LeanDojo}: Theorem proving with retrieval-augmented language models.\n658\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n659\t\n660\t\\end{thebibliography}\n661\t\n662\t\\appendix\n663\t\n664\t\\section{Hacking Taxonomy Details}\n665\t\\label{app:taxonomy}\n666\t\n667\tThe 5-mode extensional shortcut taxonomy was derived from manual annotation of 200 outputs per condition (exec-only s42, ISR $\\beta{=}0.1$ s42, random $\\beta{=}0.1$ s42).\n668\tInter-annotator agreement is not reported as annotation was performed by a single annotator.\n669\t\n670\t\\begin{itemize}\n671\t\\item \\textbf{Conclusion Embedding} (dominant in exec-only and ISR $\\beta{=}0.1$): The model includes the conclusion or its negation as an explicit premise, making the proof trivially valid. ISR for these outputs is typically $>0$ because removing other premises does not affect the proof, but removing the embedded conclusion does.\n672\t\\item \\textbf{Redundant Premises}: Multiple premises that are logically equivalent or subsumed, inflating component count without contributing to the proof. ISR $= 0$ because no individual premise is necessary.\n673\t\\item \\textbf{Tautological Collapse} (dominant in exec-only s44): The formalization reduces to a 2-component tautology ($P \\to P$). ISR is misleadingly high ($\\sim$0.5) because both components are trivially ``necessary'' for the tautological proof.\n674\t\\item \\textbf{Repetition Collapse} (dominant in exec-only s43): The model generates repetitive structure (same premise copied multiple times), collapsing prove rate. ISR $= 0$.\n675\t\\item \\textbf{Complexity Without Utility}: High component count with syntactically varied but semantically vacuous premises. Eliminated by ISR $\\beta{=}0.1$ (0\\% in ISR condition vs.\\ 3.8\\% in random condition).\n676\t\\end{itemize}\n677\t\n678\t\\section{Reproducibility Details}\n679\t\\label{app:repro}\n680\t\n681\tAll GRPO training uses the following hyperparameters: learning rate $5 \\times 10^{-6}$, group size $K{=}8$, KL coefficient $\\beta_{\\text{KL}}{=}0.01$, batch size 16, maximum sequence length 2048.\n682\tProver9 timeout is 30 seconds per call.\n683\tISR computation adds approximately $3\\times$ wall-clock time to evaluation (mean 8.5 components $\\times$ 30s timeout per ablation call, though most terminate earlier).\n684\t\n685\t\\end{document}\n686\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dcf33VCz6gsQyTv4BYFvp6","session_id":"f8da9cdc-096a-48ff-af14-645ac8499d5f","uuid":"67972554-f5e7-4c73-adf5-caf671f297e4","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:27:14.392Z"}
