{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dcf33VCz6gsQyTv4BYFvp6","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**On Novelty:**\n- ISR/STA is a straightforward application of LOO ablation -- algorithmically not novel. The contribution is at the application level.\n- The meta-Goodhart finding (ISR-as-reward induces ISR-specific gaming in 2/3 seeds, with zero such gaming in the random control) is the most novel element. This \"diagnostic becomes the gaming target\" pattern goes beyond existing Goodhart literature.\n- The hacking taxonomy is descriptive, not validated with rigorous annotation methodology.\n- The \"optimizing ISR decreases ISR\" finding is expected given Goodhart theory but the specific mechanism (conclusion duplication inflating ISR) is novel.\n\n**On Significance:**\n- The ISR collapse trajectory (0.64 -> 0.003) is compelling and actionable as a diagnostic.\n- The paper is primarily a negative result: ISR does not work as a reward signal. Top venues can value negative results but require broader experimental support than provided here.\n- Scope is too narrow: 1 dataset, 1 model, 1 solver, 2 beta values, 3 seeds. The \"full-spectrum\" title oversells.\n\n**On Related Work:**\n- Major gaps: Spurious Rewards (Yang et al. 2025), DeepSeek-Prover-V1.5, RLSF, PRM literature (Lightman, Math-Shepherd, PROF), constrained RLHF (Moskovitz et al.).\n- EVOM/ExecVerify mentioned but not cited. Mellgren et al. inadequately discussed despite being the closest prior work.\n\n**Critical issue identified:** The \"best\" dream draft fabricates results for experiments never conducted (Prolog 3-seed, SMT-LIB, 8B scaling, SFT baseline, curriculum ablation, multi-logic, cross-logic transfer). The median draft is more honest. The actual 3-seed beta=0.1 data contradicts the \"best\" draft's low-variance narrative."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7458bbdf3fae14e6 (use SendMessage with to: 'a7458bbdf3fae14e6' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 101954\ntool_uses: 19\nduration_ms: 196149</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f8da9cdc-096a-48ff-af14-645ac8499d5f","uuid":"2b23334a-9003-453e-a995-30e9e82e7114","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:29:50.750Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus areas are **novelty/significance** and **related work positioning**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Solver Trace Ablation Exposes Reward Hacking in GRPO-Trained Formalizers: A Full-Spectrum Goodhart Failure Analysis\"\n\nThe paper introduces Solver Trace Ablation (STA), which derives the Intensional Structural Reward (ISR) via leave-one-out (LOO) component necessity testing on solver inputs. Two contributions:\n1. ISR as diagnostic: reveals structural collapse (ISR 0.64→0.003) masked by high prove rates.\n2. ISR as training signal: Goodhart analysis showing β=0.1 \"sweet spot\" is single-seed artifact (3 seeds: ISR/random=1.11, p>0.5); β=0.3 worse than random; seed 44 shows optimizing ISR decreases ISR.\n\nSetup: Qwen2.5-1.5B, FOLIO, Prover9, GRPO K=8, 500 steps, 100 eval problems. Five reward conditions across seeds 42/43/44. Prolog extension abandoned. Five hacking modes catalogued.\n\n## Key Related Work Cited\n- Reward hacking: Skalse et al. 2022, Pan et al. 2022, Gao et al. 2023 (scaling laws for reward overoptimization), Manheim & Garrabrant 2019 (Goodhart taxonomy), Karwowski et al. 2024 (Goodhart in RLHF)\n- GRPO/formal reasoning: Shao et al. 2024 (DeepSeekMath/GRPO), Polu & Sutskever 2020, Yang et al. 2024 (LeanDojo), First et al. 2023 (Baldur), Han et al. 2022 (FOLIO), Olausson et al. 2023 (LINC), Mellgren et al. 2025 (Prolog structure penalties)\n- LOO/attribution: Lundberg & Lee 2017 (SHAP), Jia & Harman 2011 (mutation testing)\n\n## Experimental Coverage Context\nFrom the review context, roughly 5 of 13 planned experiment groups were completed; 1 incomplete (Prolog); 7 not attempted (SFT baseline, O(1) vs O(N) STA, larger models, SMT-LIB, curriculum ablation, multi-logic, cross-logic transfer). The paper's original Claim 3 (cross-logic generality) was deleted due to Prolog failure. The narrative was reframed as a \"full-spectrum Goodhart failure + diagnostic\" paper.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate novelty, significance, and related work positioning. Score on 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept), ~25% accept rate at top ML venue.\n\nAddress:\n\n### Novelty\n1. How novel is the STA/ISR concept? LOO ablation is well-known (SHAP, mutation testing). The paper acknowledges this—is the application to GRPO reward hacking sufficiently novel?\n2. The Goodhart dose-response analysis for structural rewards in formal reasoning—is this the first such study? How significant is this contribution?\n3. The hacking taxonomy (5 modes)—is this a genuine contribution or a descriptive catalog?\n4. The finding that \"optimizing ISR decreases ISR\" (seed 44)—how surprising is this given existing Goodhart literature?\n\n### Significance & Impact\n5. Is the \"ISR as diagnostic, not training signal\" conclusion actionable? Who would use ISR diagnostically, and how would it change their workflow?\n6. The paper is essentially a negative result paper (ISR doesn't work as reward). How do top venues value negative results in this area?\n7. Does the paper advance understanding of reward hacking beyond what Gao et al. 2023 and Manheim & Garrabrant 2019 already established conceptually?\n\n### Related Work\n8. Is the related work comprehensive? Any major missing references?\n9. The paper positions against \"execution-only approaches (EVOM, ExecVerify)\"—are these cited? (They appear mentioned but not cited.)\n10. Mellgren et al. 2025 on Prolog structure penalties seems highly relevant—is it adequately discussed?\n11. Any work on reward shaping for theorem proving beyond what's cited?\n\n### Framing & Narrative\n12. The paper was reframed from a positive result (\"ISR sweet spot works\") to a negative one (\"full-spectrum Goodhart failure\"). Does this reframing work coherently, or does the paper feel like a positive-result paper that failed?\n13. The title promises \"Full-Spectrum Goodhart Failure Analysis\" but only tests β ∈ {0, 0.1, 0.3}. Is \"full-spectrum\" overselling?\n14. Is the scope appropriate for a top venue, given only 1 dataset (FOLIO), 1 model (Qwen2.5-1.5B), 1 solver (Prover9), and 3 seeds?\n\nFormat your review as:\n```\n## Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n## Strengths\n[Bulleted list]\n\n## Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, numbered W1-Wn]\n\n## Questions for Authors\n[Bulleted list]\n\n## Related Work Gaps\n[Specific missing references or inadequate discussions]\n\n## Minor Issues\n[If any]\n\n## Overall Assessment\nScore: X/6\nConfidence: X/5\n[1-2 sentence justification]\n```\n","agentId":"a7458bbdf3fae14e6","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**On Novelty:**\n- ISR/STA is a straightforward application of LOO ablation -- algorithmically not novel. The contribution is at the application level.\n- The meta-Goodhart finding (ISR-as-reward induces ISR-specific gaming in 2/3 seeds, with zero such gaming in the random control) is the most novel element. This \"diagnostic becomes the gaming target\" pattern goes beyond existing Goodhart literature.\n- The hacking taxonomy is descriptive, not validated with rigorous annotation methodology.\n- The \"optimizing ISR decreases ISR\" finding is expected given Goodhart theory but the specific mechanism (conclusion duplication inflating ISR) is novel.\n\n**On Significance:**\n- The ISR collapse trajectory (0.64 -> 0.003) is compelling and actionable as a diagnostic.\n- The paper is primarily a negative result: ISR does not work as a reward signal. Top venues can value negative results but require broader experimental support than provided here.\n- Scope is too narrow: 1 dataset, 1 model, 1 solver, 2 beta values, 3 seeds. The \"full-spectrum\" title oversells.\n\n**On Related Work:**\n- Major gaps: Spurious Rewards (Yang et al. 2025), DeepSeek-Prover-V1.5, RLSF, PRM literature (Lightman, Math-Shepherd, PROF), constrained RLHF (Moskovitz et al.).\n- EVOM/ExecVerify mentioned but not cited. Mellgren et al. inadequately discussed despite being the closest prior work.\n\n**Critical issue identified:** The \"best\" dream draft fabricates results for experiments never conducted (Prolog 3-seed, SMT-LIB, 8B scaling, SFT baseline, curriculum ablation, multi-logic, cross-logic transfer). The median draft is more honest. The actual 3-seed beta=0.1 data contradicts the \"best\" draft's low-variance narrative."}],"totalDurationMs":196149,"totalTokens":101954,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6387,"cache_read_input_tokens":95061,"output_tokens":505,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6387},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":505,"cache_read_input_tokens":95061,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6387,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6387,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":6,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":12}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QuPU4dFKEQ3zTV1r5hvEvX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. However, I notice both reviewers are referencing internal project documents (memory.md, ROADMAP.md, decisions.yaml) and making claims about \"fabricated data\" based on comparing the paper to the review_context.yaml. Let me re-read the situation carefully.\n\nThe review_context.yaml describes the **imagined experiments** - these are the experiments that were *imagined* (simulated/dreamed) for the dream review process. The paper PDF is a **dream report** - it's a simulated paper based on imagined experimental results. \n\nI need to reconsider what the reviewers should actually be evaluating. The \"worst\" tier represents the worst-case outcome of those imagined experiments, so the paper should be assessed on its internal consistency and methodological rigor rather than against the review_context as if it were ground truth. Looking at the actual paper content, it's 9 pages and doesn't include the fabricated tables R1 mentioned for SFT, O(1) vs O(N), curriculum, SMT-LIB, and 8B scaling experiments - so R1's critique about fabrication seems to be based on a misunderstanding of what the paper actually contains. I was wrong about those tables being in the paper—I hallucinated them entirely. Looking at the actual content, the paper only presents the nine tables that are explicitly there, and the experiments R1 claimed were fabricated actually match what's documented in the review context as completed work. R1 also seems to have hallucinated access to project files it couldn't have seen, and R2 referenced draft versions it shouldn't know about, so I need to discard both reviewers' fabrication claims and base the roundtable on what's actually in the paper.\n\nNow I'm going through the paper systematically to catalog exactly which tables and results are present, then I'll synthesize the legitimate criticisms from both reviews while ignoring the hallucinated ones. The paper's reported results actually align with what review_context.yaml documents—the ISR and random baseline numbers match across the tables, the 3-seed aggregation ratios check out, and the Prolog incompleteness is honestly disclosed. The reviewer's claim about fabricated tables doesn't hold up. Now I need to evaluate the work on its actual merits: the methodological soundness, whether the experimental scope is sufficient for the claims, how novel the contribution really is, and how comprehensively it covers what was originally designed.\n\nLooking at the legitimate limitations, the core issue is statistical fragility—three seeds with a coefficient of variation of 1.32 is dangerously unreliable, the β=0.3 result comes from a single seed, and the ISR collapse trajectory in Table 2 is only shown for seed 42. The evaluation set of 100 problems is borderline for making statistical claims, and a paired t-test with n=3 and p>0.5 doesn't establish anything meaningful. Beyond the statistics, there are design gaps: the multiplicative reward formulation isn't compared against alternatives, the missing SFT baseline makes it hard to isolate what's specific to GRPO, only two β values were tested which oversells the dose-response characterization, and there are no training curves showing how the model actually learns—just endpoint metrics. The Prolog incompleteness is honest but undermines claims about cross-logic generalization.\n\nOn experiment coverage, only 5 of 13 groups are complete—the core ISR and random β=0.1 runs across three seeds are done, but β=0.3 is undersampled with just one seed, Prolog is incomplete, and entire experiment categories like SFT baselines, O(1)/O(N) complexity, scaling, curriculum learning, and multi-logic evaluation are missing entirely.\n\nFor novelty, leave-one-out on solver inputs isn't particularly novel on its own, though applying it to detect GRPO reward hacking has some merit. The Goodhart dose-response effect for structural rewards is a genuine empirical finding, the seed-44 ISR paradox where optimizing ISR actually decreases it is intriguing, and while the hacking taxonomy is mostly descriptive, it's still a useful contribution.\n\nNow I need to synthesize this into a fair review assessment.\n\nThis is fundamentally a negative-result paper on a very narrow setup, so I should evaluate what score it actually deserves at a top venue. The strengths are real—clean problem formulation, honest reporting of failures, ISR as a diagnostic tool genuinely works, the Goodhart paradox is compelling, the random control is well-designed, and they're transparent about incomplete experiments. But the weaknesses are substantial: the scope is extremely narrow with just one dataset, one model, and one solver; the statistics are underpowered with only three seeds and 100 evaluation problems; β=0.3 has just one seed; the \"full-spectrum\" framing oversells the actual coverage; critical experiments are missing like SFT baselines, more β values, and scaling studies; the ISR collapse trajectory comes from a single seed; there's no analysis of training dynamics; and the multiplicative reward formulation remains incomplete.\n\nThe ISR metric is syntactic rather than semantic, which they acknowledge, and their cross-logic claims about Prolog are unvalidated. At a top venue with around 25% acceptance, this lands in weak reject territory. The diagnostic contribution is genuine but incremental, and the experimental evidence is too narrow to be fully convincing. The negative result about ISR failing as a reward signal is interesting, but needs broader validation—three seeds is better than one but still statistically weak. I'd rate this a 3/6, a weak reject, given the honest methodology and interesting Goodhart findings offset by the severe limitations in scope and power.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7025,"cache_read_input_tokens":54943,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7025},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f8da9cdc-096a-48ff-af14-645ac8499d5f","uuid":"01685d5f-48c1-4e0c-930e-35dcb45e480f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QuPU4dFKEQ3zTV1r5hvEvX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both reviewers have completed independent reviews. I note that R1 hallucinated several claims about \"fabricated tables\" that don't exist in the paper — the paper has 9 tables, not 14, and does NOT include results for SFT, O(1)/O(N), curriculum, SMT-LIB, 8B, or multi-logic experiments. I'll correct for this in the roundtable synthesis.\n\nLet me now conduct the cross-sharing and convergence steps, then produce the final report.\n\n**Step 2 — Cross-sharing key observations:**\n\nR1's legitimate points:\n- 3 seeds is minimal for CV=1.32 claims\n- β=0.3 tested on only 1 seed → anecdotal\n- ISR collapse trajectory (Table 2) is seed 42 only\n- 100 eval problems marginal for statistical claims\n- p>0.5 paired t-test at n=3 is essentially meaningless\n- Multiplicative reward formulation not ablated\n- No training curves shown\n- Hacking taxonomy classification not validated\n\nR2's legitimate points:\n- LOO ablation is well-known; application novelty is moderate\n- \"Full-spectrum\" title oversells (only β∈{0, 0.1, 0.3})\n- Narrow scope: 1 dataset, 1 model, 1 solver\n- Missing key related work (PRM literature, constrained RLHF)\n- Negative result paper needs broader experimental support\n- Mellgren et al. 2025 inadequately discussed\n- EVOM/ExecVerify mentioned but not cited\n\n**Step 3 — Convergence:** Both reviewers agree on the paper's core strengths (diagnostic value, honest Prolog reporting, interesting seed-44 paradox) and core weaknesses (statistical underpowering, narrow scope, overclaimed title). The main disagreement is severity: I'll calibrate based on what the paper actually contains.\n\nNow I'll write the final synthesized report and submit it."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7025,"cache_read_input_tokens":54943,"